WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.800
- My name is Leslie Gross-Wyrtzen

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.640
and I am an affiliate
in the McMillan Center

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.211
in the program for African
Studies and Middle East studies.

00:00:14.211 --> 00:00:17.160
I've also been involved with
the Program for Refugees,

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.151
Forced Displacement,
Humanitarian Response,

00:00:20.151 --> 00:00:22.950
and a fellow for the Center

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.010
for Race, Indigeneity, and
Transnational Migration.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.480
I remembered all of those things.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.020
So, it is a real pleasure today

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.110
to introduce Dr. Heba Gowayed.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.793
I'm gonna say it the long way,

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.580
who is an assistant professor

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.680
of sociology at Boston University

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.930
and currently a member of the Institute

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:46.104
for Advanced Study at
Princeton and her research,

00:00:46.104 --> 00:00:49.110
which is global and comparative examines

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.368
how low income people
traverse social services,

00:00:52.368 --> 00:00:55.980
immigration laws, and their
associated bureaucracies,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.310
while grappling with gender
and racial inequalities.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:00.733
And she's published on these topics

00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:04.443
as well as health and
education, as I discovered,

00:01:05.802 --> 00:01:09.330
educational access for folks from Cairo

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.120
in race and ethnic studies,
Sociological Forum,

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.420
Gender and Society in the
American Behavioral Scientist.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:18.120
And today she's gonna talk
about her book "Refuge,"

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.850
which was published by
Princeton University Press,

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.340
which is an ethnography
that explores the lives

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.320
of Syrian seeking refuge in

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.930
the US, in Canada, and in Germany.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.000
And the book, one thing
that's really exciting

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.200
about having her back here is

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.600
that the book began in
New Haven, Connecticut,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.960
right here where Heba was
living and volunteering

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.380
among recently resettled Syrian families.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.630
So, in the book, she
examines whether and how

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.940
these countries recognize and invest

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.880
in new arrivals humanity and potential,

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.300
shaping their economic realities
and feelings of belonging.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.980
As countries receive refugees

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.637
through their social welfare systems,

00:01:54.637 --> 00:01:56.550
"Refuge" raises a mirror to how

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.910
these systems reproduce social inequality.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.170
And if we have time,

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.270
we might hear a little bit
about her next project,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.980
which is called "The Cost of Borders,"

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.280
which thinks about borders
and border enforcement

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.000
as a series of costly
and deadly transactions.

