There we go, we're good.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.770
Okay, so thank you all
so much for coming today.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.290
I'm gonna talk to you
about my book "Refuge,

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.230
How the State Shapes Human Potential."

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:22.713
That it was out as of April of this year.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.720
Okay, so as you all know
today, the world is facing one

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.870
of the largest displacement
crises in modern history.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.990
And this is due to longstanding
realities of local violence

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.269
produced by colonial legacies as well

00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:41.850
as newer facts of
environmental degradation

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.431
and the disappearance
of land and resources.

00:03:44.431 --> 00:03:46.830
82.4 million people,

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:49.320
more than 1% of the world's populations,

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.640
one in every 95 people are
currently forcibly displaced.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.100
And among them are 25 million people

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.010
who are displaced outside
of their country of origin

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.790
and among them, 6.7 million
or an estimated 6.7 million

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:05.610
are Syrian refugees, and
you know, despite the fact

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:09.000
that this global crisis
is ongoing, is worsening,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.003
we're really just beginning to
understand its ramifications.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.040
And so in this presentation,
I'm sharing with you

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.980
the findings of the comparative
ethnographic project

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.630
that followed Syrians as they began

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.540
new lives in the United
States, Canada and Germany,

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.180
countries that offered them
a resettlement and asylum,

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:30.180
which the United Nations
identifies as durable solutions

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.030
to the problem of displacement
and in this project,

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.640
I focus on the experience
of displaced people

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.500
who are skilled or semi-skilled,

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.840
but not particularly highly credentialed,

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.460
and who arrived each of these countries

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.400
between 2015 and 2016.

00:04:46.827 --> 00:04:49.380
And I'm gonna argue that human capital,

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.780
which we typically think
of as a crucial determinant

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:53.828
of immigrant outcomes,
is itself structured

00:04:53.828 --> 00:04:56.610
through state incorporation policy.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.950
Further, I show that the
process of its production

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:01.170
is both gendered and racialized.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.030
But before I expand on this argument,

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.980
let me first give you a
background on how these men

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.350
and women arrived to these
countries in the first place.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.440
How they became refugees,
which is also an explanation

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.890
of their selection into the
three cases that I study.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.560
So, the Syrian refugees in
this study lived in cities

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.660
like Homs, Syria's third largest,

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.910
where this covered market once stood,

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:24.390
they became refugees due
to a war that began in 2011

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.120
as a result of the
government's violent response

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.370
to civil protests for social change.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.930
The Syrian manifestation of what came

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:34.380
to be known regionally as the Arab Spring.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.390
The war took with it the sight and sounds

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.970
of the neighborhoods where
their families had lived

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.542
and the homes that they had built

00:05:40.542 --> 00:05:42.840
and the lives of their loved ones.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.500
And at first, people displaced
fled to areas nearby,

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.460
but eventually many of them
fled the country altogether.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:52.620
And most initially crossed into countries

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.630
of immediate refuge,
countries like Jordan,

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.580
Turkey and Lebanon, which continued

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.520
to host about 83% of all Syrian refugees.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.440
And there they registered with

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:04.650
the United Nations higher
council for refugees.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.210
And the thing about this is that you know,

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:09.504
you have a country like
Lebanon with 6 million people

00:06:09.504 --> 00:06:12.180
and a million Syrian
refugees arrive there.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.790
And so this of course creates
tension with local residents.

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:18.960
Politicians are using it to
to foment, you know, conflict.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.690
And so, and also there
are not opportunities,

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.400
strong opportunities for education,

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:27.120
strong opportunities for
healthcare and so as a result,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.460
a lot of people felt that
there wasn't a future there.

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.670
And for those people, there
were two possible solutions.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.433
One is resettlement and
the other is asylum.

00:06:37.775 --> 00:06:40.320
And resettlement is
where a displaced person

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.870
is selected for travel to a third country.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:45.510
They're selected based on
a measure of vulnerability.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.820
So, things like financial burdens,

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:49.350
the number of school-aged children,

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.090
whether or not there's
a chronic health burden

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:53.753
in the household or
violence and exploitation

00:06:53.753 --> 00:06:56.280
in those countries of immediate refuge.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.320
And when offered
resettlement to a country,

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.550
they can say yes or no,

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:02.910
but they cannot choose
the destination itself.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:05.250
Refugees are then vetted
for travel through

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:09.000
an arduous process that can
take approximately two years.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.890
The United States and Canada led the world

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.050
on resettlement, a program that takes in

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.600
hundreds of thousands of people each year.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.727
However, given the
sheer number of refugees

00:07:17.727 --> 00:07:19.590
who are displaced, globally,

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.510
resettlement is a super rare solution.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:26.100
So, only 1% of all displaced
people will ever be resettled.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.200
So, for those not offered resettlement,

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.350
the other solution to
displacement is asylum.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.540
And I'm sure many of you remember watching

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:36.960
in the summer of 2015 as
almost a million people

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.820
with the help of smugglers made

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.770
the treacherous journey in blow up rafts

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.710
across the Mediterranean
Sea and into Europe.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:47.047
As one Syrian woman Welat
put it to me rhetorically,

00:07:47.047 --> 00:07:48.810
"Do you know what it is to go into debt

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.750
to put your children
in the arms of death?"

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.900
Asylum is where a refugee
stakes his or her claim

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.090
by going to a country
that could offer them

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:58.950
legal status and applying.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:00.630
By some estimates, Germany took in

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:04.470
about half a million Syrian
asylees between 2015 and 2016.

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:07.440
And recent statistic
that I looked up says 1%

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.173
of all of the German
population right now is Syrian.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.250
So, resettlement and asylum
offer those seeking refuge

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:17.070
an opportunity to rebuild
their lives in well equipped,

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:19.560
wealthy countries with full legal status.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.560
But what do these institutional
solutions mean for people

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.440
who have experienced such
traumatic displacement

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.040
and are embarking on new
beginnings in strange countries?

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.200
Today, we're gonna look at
resettlement and asylum,

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.140
not as ends, but as
continuations to the process

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:37.170
of displacement and seeking refuge.

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:39.120
Indeed, by the time
that the Syrian refugees

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.070
in this study had arrived
to the United States,

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:43.260
Canada or Germany, they'd experienced

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.060
a number of significant losses.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.470
They'd lost their country,

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:47.738
arriving to a new one where their,

00:08:47.738 --> 00:08:48.870
(Heba speaking foreign language)

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:52.080
or norms and traditions
were entirely different,

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:53.850
whose language was not their mother tongue

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:54.930
and where they would be ethnic,

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:56.610
religious and racial minorities

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.043
stigmatized by association to terrorism.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.490
They had resigned themselves
to not seeing their family

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:05.640
for the foreseeable future, if ever again.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:07.320
But what's more they would experience

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:09.330
the dimming of their potential.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:10.620
In countries of resettlement

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.840
and asylum, their skills and abilities,

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.630
what we typically identify
as their human capital

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:19.620
did not have the same
residence as they did in Syria.

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.150
The focus of this presentation

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:23.703
is on why that residence is lost.

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:29.580
So, the term human
capital, central to studies

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:32.010
in economics or public health or you know,

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:35.400
all of the social sciences
refers to individual abilities

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:38.130
and qualities that make
workers productive.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:40.710
Sociologists have shown that human capital

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:42.510
is an important determinant of immigrant

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:45.180
socioeconomic mobility alongside

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:47.250
the characteristics of
a receiving context,

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:50.403
particularly for the first
group of immigrants who arrives.

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:53.790
In other words, this literature
shows that human capital

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:56.673
and context together
shape immigrant outcomes.

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.570
Human capital is typically
measured cross sectionally

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:03.720
as years of education or
occupational histories.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:06.300
It's imagined as something that
immigrants bring with them,

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:09.540
an independent variable
that shapes other outcomes.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:11.580
However, this leaves the question,

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:15.210
if one had five years of Syria
in public school education

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.880
or held an occupation
as an artisan in Syria,

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:20.130
how does this actually
matter for their productivity

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.200
in a destination economy?

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:23.970
This question has been of interest

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:26.160
to economists by
identifying what they call

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:28.710
the imperfect transferability
of human capital

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:31.110
for immigrants who are moving
across disparate context.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:34.890
It's also been of interest to
others who identify migration

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.623
as a process as disruptive
of human capital.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.490
Some have tried to
explain this discrepancy.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:46.747
Human capital theorists simply say,

00:10:46.747 --> 00:10:50.130
"Well, you must not have the
necessary skills, right?"

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:52.320
Signaling theorists argue
that that doesn't really make

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:55.410
sense because skills are not
a recognizable thing, right?

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:58.200
It depends on whether or not they're seen

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:01.041
or whether or not people are
understood to have skills.

00:11:01.041 --> 00:11:03.000
So, it might be that you just don't have

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.190
the credentials that
are legible to employers

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:07.770
regardless of the skills
you actually hold.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:09.480
Yet other theorists show that the reading

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:11.490
of skills is shaped by racism, right?

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.230
Or that the skills of minoritized groups

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:15.990
are necessarily always seen as inferior.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:18.780
However, these explanations
continue to treat the immigrant

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:21.870
as a puzzle piece entering
the labor market puzzle.

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:23.310
But what of the state that governs

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:25.443
the entire process of migration?

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:29.220
So, in this study I introduce a theory

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.500
of state structured human capital.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.390
I argue looking at the early period

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.030
and through a comparative
angle that human capital

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:39.120
can be created, transformed,
or destroyed through

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.970
the incorporation policies
of receiving countries,

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:45.510
which derived from national
approaches to social welfare.

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:49.200
I find that incorporation systems
can intervene in two ways.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.350
First, they can recognize or
ignore immigrant histories,

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:54.450
seeing people unskilled who may in fact

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:56.370
have skills that could be activated.

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:58.770
This has always mattered for
highly skilled immigrants

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.450
like doctors or lawyers.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.420
But also in this study
I will talk about Amin,

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:05.760
who arrives in Germany as a truck driver

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.190
and he's worked his whole
life as a truck driver.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:10.680
I show that this history of
him working as a truck driver

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:13.260
only matters in the German
labor market as long as

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:17.490
he's recognized as being
a truck driver in Germany.

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:20.640
Second, states can invest in
newcomer refugee skills so

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:23.160
that they can translate their
histories in ways that are

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:25.110
productive in a new setting or enable them

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:27.840
to acquire skills that
matter in that setting.

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.480
For instance, states can provide
language learning classes

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.030
or teach refugees in new
skill that is needed in

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:36.360
the receiving economy but that
they did not previously have.

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.310
It can also provide financial assistance

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:40.380
to allow refugees to benefit from existing

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:43.413
skill building opportunities
in the context of reception.

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:48.030
So, for these reasons I argue
that human capital is not

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.180
only a determinant of immigrant
outcomes but an outcome

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:53.523
structured by state
incorporation policies.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:58.500
I also show that the production
of human capital is gender.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:01.500
As scholars of gender have
shown institutions and services

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:05.490
themselves feature gendered
expectations of who is a worker.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:09.060
These interact with the
Syrian's own gender expectations

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:11.490
where men are often seen
as primary breadwinners

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:12.930
resulting in different, as I will show

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:15.303
some surprising outcomes
for women and men.

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:18.630
I also show that the production

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:20.820
of human capital is racialized.

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:23.490
So, countries equip new
arrivals in their pursuit

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:26.130
of a good and stable life
through the very same

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:28.470
racist social welfare
systems that have long shaped

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:30.750
the contours of national belonging.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:33.300
But what's more, as
scholars of race have shown,

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.160
discrimination and racist violence

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.770
diminishes opportunities
for engaging in the society

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:40.380
in which one finds themselves
so minoritized people

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.450
may fear being in public space.

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:44.100
And this was particularly true for women

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:45.843
who wore the hijab in this study.

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:51.150
So, now turning briefly to a
theory on immigrant reception,

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:53.460
foundational literature
and sociology holds

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:55.080
that immigrant welcome is contingent

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.550
on determinants including things

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:59.460
like race, social capital or legal status.

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:00.990
The focus of this literature tends

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:03.240
to be on long term integration

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.510
or how immigrants fit
into a national whole.

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:09.810
Instead, I join others who
have shifted the question,

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:13.320
focusing not on how immigrants
fit into a given context,

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:16.410
but how context works in
the lives of immigrants.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:19.110
I focus on the under study
crucial initial years

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:22.170
in which newcomer histories
collide with new systems

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:25.260
to coalesce into their
first designations of race,

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:28.020
of legal status and of human capital.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.490
And this early period is crucial

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:31.500
because it shapes where
immigrants start, right?

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:34.980
And how they then progress in
these destination contacts.

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:38.310
I focus specifically on
social welfare policies which

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:41.340
have long again shaped the
contours of national belonging

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:44.610
and from which social assistance
for immigrants derives.

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:47.190
Finally, I compare refugees
who because they receive

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:49.380
social assistance denied
to other categories

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:51.810
of immigrants due to
their legal recognition

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:54.810
as refugees are a
conservative case to explore

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:57.483
how incorporation policies
matter for new arrivals.

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:01.773
But before we get to how
refugees are incorporated

00:15:01.773 --> 00:15:05.040
through resettlement
policies, the men and women

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:07.470
in this study have to
first secure legal status.

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:09.180
Let's talk slightly about arrival.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:12.510
So, in the United States and
Canada, recognized refugees

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:15.270
are admitted per long
time resettlement systems.

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.430
And on your screen is
the approximate number

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:19.020
of Syrian refugees taken in by

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:22.200
each country between 2015 and 2017.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:23.160
Germany, as you see,

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:25.560
took in at least half a million refugees.

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:27.300
And this was due to the suspension,

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.280
the temporary suspension
of the Dublin regulation

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:31.320
by Anglo Merkel that would've
otherwise kept people

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.170
at the borders of the European Union.

00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:36.780
And legally in the
United States and Canada,

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:38.430
refugees have permanent residency

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.305
within a year of their arrival.

00:15:40.305 --> 00:15:42.098
But in Germany they receive a one

00:15:42.098 --> 00:15:44.010
to three year renewable residency

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:45.597
depending on their level
of protection, right?

00:15:45.597 --> 00:15:47.583
And we could talk about these later.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:50.970
We're gonna keep it light for
this part of the presentation.

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:52.920
Okay, so what are these countries

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.350
incorporation policies for refugees?

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:56.760
So, each derives again from the

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:58.470
social welfare system of the state.

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:00.030
So, in the United States, as you all know,

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:02.040
we have a liberal market economy.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:03.840
And as one feature the United States

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:05.496
has limited social service system

00:16:05.496 --> 00:16:08.340
because it treats poverty
as an individual failure

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:10.050
as argued for instance by Michael Katz

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:11.730
in "The Undeserving Poor."

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:14.640
In her work, Sibil Fox shows
that this approach to poverty

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:16.860
is inextricably linked
to the disenfranchisement

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.530
of black Americans in the United States.

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:23.490
Refugees only regular cash
assistance comes from TANF,

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.950
which is Temporary Assistance
for Needy Families.

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:27.570
The national welfare system offered

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:29.640
to low income people in the country.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:32.460
Introduced in '96, the
program has lifetime limits

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:34.590
and offers low amounts of cash.

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:36.930
This design matches the goal of both TANF

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:40.410
and the 1980 Refugee Acts,
which is for self-sufficiency,

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:42.210
that the low income refugees included

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:43.440
should not be relying on
government assistance.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:45.390
So, it's important to
note here that there are

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.360
other programs like Match
Program or like RCA,

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:50.640
but there are all sort of
limited in what the assistance

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:52.040
that they offer to refugees.

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:57.000
Canada, the second case is
also a liberal market economy,

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:58.260
but characterized by what's been

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:00.450
called inclusive liberalism.

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:02.700
So, what this means is that
the social service system

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.150
in Canada is generous,
providing Canadians who need it

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:09.060
with enough assistance to
cover their basic needs.

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:10.920
And this generosity
shapes the government's

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:14.010
proactive approach to
immigrant incorporation.

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:16.620
But that proactive approach is
also facilitated by the fact

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:18.840
that Canada has way more
restrictive policies

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:20.730
for admission than the
United States does, right?

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:23.880
So, it's whoever gets
beyond the, you know,

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:26.790
the welcome mat surrounded by nails,

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:29.400
as one writer put it gets this assistance.

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:31.920
But everybody else is sort of kept out.

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:33.960
And as Irene Bloemraad shows in her work,

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:35.970
immigrants and refugees
are received through

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:38.730
what's called state
sponsored multiculturalism.

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:40.920
Which means that Canada
actively recruits and seeks

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:43.590
to integrate immigrants into
the fabric of their society

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:46.620
by recognizing them as
ethnic minorities providing

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:50.280
instrumental and symbolic
support to them in that category.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:51.690
And so it's with this express goal

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.750
of integration that
refugees there are met.

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:57.720
Finally, Germany is a
corporatist system which means

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:00.450
that the national economy
is organized by coordination

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:03.270
between the state, the
unions and employers

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:05.820
to the perceived benefit of all three.

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:08.280
So, the system means
there's really high levels

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:10.380
of expenditure on social programs

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:12.510
and a highly centralized
and powerful state,

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:13.860
which Myer Marksfried, for instance,

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.440
described as producing the German nation.

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:19.410
And this nation was long
comprised of ethnic Germans.

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:22.320
As Germany has a long history
of excluding, you know,

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:25.860
violently non-German people
from full integration.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:29.100
Syrians entering the system
benefit from the generous

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:32.490
financial assistance that
exists for the German state.

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:35.790
However, it comes with
really meaningful conditions.

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:37.077
So, to enter the labor market,

00:18:37.077 --> 00:18:39.270
which is condition of permanent residency,

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:42.060
you need to first excel in German classes

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:44.280
and then you need to
earn a German credential,

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:46.980
as do 85% of German workers.

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:48.777
So, as a result I call the asylum program

00:18:48.777 --> 00:18:51.990
in Germany one of credentialization.

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:55.290
Okay, so how do refugees
experience these three systems?

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:56.490
Before I get into the data,

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.650
let me give you an
explanation of my methods

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:01.170
and a little more on key selection.

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:03.450
So, to compile the neat
data chart that you see

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:06.300
on the chart before you
involved in ethnography

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:09.213
conducted in three countries
over the span of three years,

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:12.810
I'm fluent in Arabic and in 2015

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:14.649
I was kicked outta my field site in Egypt.

00:19:14.649 --> 00:19:16.770
The long term consequences of another

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:19.590
dictatorial backlash
against the Arab Spring.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:20.760
I was living in Connecticut

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:21.963
when the United States here, right?

00:19:21.963 --> 00:19:24.300
In New Haven, when the United States

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:25.590
began to resettle refugees

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:28.410
to a local resettlement agency in 2015.

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:30.450
And I volunteered as an interpreter there

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:32.550
where I met the 18 families in my project

00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:34.890
who I got to know closely
in the coming years,

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:36.810
observing firsthand meetings they had

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:38.340
with the Department of Social Services,

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:41.730
negotiations with off the books
employers and interactions

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:44.837
with the resettlement agency,
which is also local, sorry.

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:52.620
So, and I recognize as I spoke to these,

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:55.110
you know, to these new
arrivals, how important

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:57.410
the United States Social
Services were to the territory.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.980
Though they were living in a liberal city

00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:03.420
with strong social services compared

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:04.530
to elsewhere in the United States,

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:07.200
in a very strong
resettlement agency as well,

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:08.970
they had tremendous trepidation about

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:11.580
their assistance and
also about finding jobs.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:12.510
They were living under the

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:15.150
constant threat of American poverty.

00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:17.490
I wondered how the
experiences would differ

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:19.950
if they had arrived to another country.

00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:22.170
And at that point, Justin
Trudeau, who had run on

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:26.040
a platform of resettling
25,000 refugees to Canada,

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:29.070
won the premiership and was
making good on his promise.

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:31.890
And flights to Canada at
the time were inexpensive.

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:33.750
So, I got to Toronto in February

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:35.640
'cause I was a graduate
student with no money,

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:38.400
so I got to Toronto in
February in time to see

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:41.640
thousands of people crammed
into hotels and motels

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:44.130
and government offices trying to rent cars

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:45.570
to go apartment searching in what was

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:48.090
a brutally cold Canadian winter.

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:50.310
And so there I include
five families, two of whom

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:52.530
are related to families in
New Haven and all of whom

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:55.140
are selected on the basis of
the same selection process

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:56.880
of those arriving to the United States,

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:59.180
making them good comparative cases.

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:02.250
While Canada has a private
sponsorship program,

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:04.680
I include people selected
by a government sponsorship

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:06.270
for the sake of comparability.

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:08.070
Though two of the families
did receive private

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:10.260
sponsorship support for six months as part

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:11.790
of a hybrid program, which again I'm happy

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:13.500
to talk about in the Q and A.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:15.960
And initially I visited Canada for a month

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:18.450
and I returned every
six months for two weeks

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:19.916
and maintained contact with
these families longitudinal,

00:21:19.916 --> 00:21:23.431
vis-a-vis social media, et cetera.

00:21:23.431 --> 00:21:25.830
I also did some focus
groups and participated

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:27.390
in some focus groups that
were being done there just

00:21:27.390 --> 00:21:30.990
to triangulate this data
against a bigger populations.

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:32.460
Finally, with the help of the NSF

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.590
and the Horowitz Foundation,
I included the case

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:36.210
of Germany due to the sheer number

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:38.130
of refugees who were admitted there.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:39.540
And because it's a welfare state

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:41.100
that's predicated on very different

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:43.560
integration approaches I just described.

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:45.764
So, in the summer of 2017,
I included five families

00:21:45.764 --> 00:21:47.940
who were related elsewhere my study.

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:50.550
But because this research
would not be longitudinal,

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:52.170
I returned in the summer of 2018

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:54.118
to conduct additional interviews,

00:21:54.118 --> 00:21:56.490
a sample of which were a sample of the

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:59.670
University of Constance's
National Survey on Refugees,

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:01.830
which at that time was just being launched

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:04.380
and these were mostly
conducted in Bavaria.

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:05.640
So, regarding the study sample,

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:08.790
as you'll see in the United
States, Canada and Germany,

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:11.550
I compare refugees from
similar backgrounds,

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:15.630
the men were truck drivers,
chefs, house painters,

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:17.940
working skilled or
semi-skilled occupations

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:19.620
but without strong formal credentials.

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:21.639
Now of course refugees from any place

00:22:21.639 --> 00:22:23.130
are a diverse group of people.

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:24.480
There are high earning refugees,

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:25.313
there are low earning refugees,

00:22:25.313 --> 00:22:28.376
but this I focus on this
specific group, okay?

00:22:28.376 --> 00:22:30.660
And the women for the
most part, as I will show,

00:22:30.660 --> 00:22:32.100
were also not employed in Syria

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:33.900
with a few notable exceptions.

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:36.090
However, in the German interviews I also

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:38.100
did interview some high earning refugees

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:39.300
just for the sake of comparability.

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:40.810
So, I am happy to talk about that.

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:42.990
There are some doctors and some lawyers.

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:46.200
Okay, so now let's turn
to what these refugees

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:48.090
reveal about the incorporation policy

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:50.140
in the United States, Canada and Germany.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:56.250
So, to organize findings
in each case I describe

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:58.740
how refugees experience their arrival

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:01.260
and the assistance that they
received from the state.

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:04.290
I then explore how each system
structured the human capital

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:06.420
of these newly arrived refugees and how

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:09.540
that experience differed
for women and for men.

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:11.640
And I began with the
case of the United States

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:14.010
and how men and women
resettled here adjusted

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:17.670
to the self-sufficiency
imperative of resettlement policy.

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:20.190
Here I highlight the case
of Rojette and Mejed,

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.950
who just seven years
prior to their arrival

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:25.260
were a middle class couple
living in Homs, Syria,

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:26.370
just around the corner from the

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:28.740
covered market that I showed you earlier.

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.522
In 2010, they finally achieved their dream

00:23:30.522 --> 00:23:32.580
of building their own home,

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:34.200
leaving the foundations in place to build

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:36.660
additional floors for each
of their three children,

00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:39.270
the oldest of whom was
a preteen at the time.

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:42.120
Their enjoyment of this dream,
however, was short lived.

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:44.040
In 2011, they fled to Jordan where Mejed

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:46.410
who had owned his own restaurant became

00:23:46.410 --> 00:23:49.380
an undocumented worker at a Shwarma stand.

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:51.180
This couple along with
their three children

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:54.060
arrived in JFK in October of 2015.

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:56.850
An international organization
for migration van

00:23:56.850 --> 00:23:59.910
drove them from JFK to
New Haven, Connecticut.

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:03.030
They were dropped off in
a dimly lit parking lot

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:05.490
of an off track betting
facility at the edge of town,

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:06.693
known as Sports Haven.

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:09.960
They piled into the car
of the resettlement agency

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:11.250
case worker and made their way

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:14.640
to a low income neighborhood
of the post-industrial city.

00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:16.770
Their apartment was on the
second floor of what used

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:19.001
to be a single family home
and they would soon realize

00:24:19.001 --> 00:24:21.870
it had a mouse infestation
and there the couple

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:23.520
would be visited by a case worker

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:26.340
who would tell them what
their assistance would be.

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:28.380
During this meeting
they learned about TANF

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:31.080
for the first time, the welfare system.

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:33.420
They would receive $803 per month,

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:35.610
the benefit for a family of five.

00:24:35.610 --> 00:24:37.470
They would also receive comparable amounts

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:40.050
in food stamps through an
electronic benefit transfer,

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:43.020
EBT, that could go towards food items.

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:44.910
At this meeting they also
learned that their rent

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:46.460
was a thousand dollars a month.

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:49.980
Here's a budget sheet for another family

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:51.540
that's early in their resettlement.

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:53.190
So, because people don't read English,

00:24:53.190 --> 00:24:55.560
on the left is a clipper of the expense

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:57.300
and each column header is the month,

00:24:57.300 --> 00:25:00.210
which his family translated
into their Arabic names.

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:02.289
Yellow highlighter is the welcome money,

00:25:02.289 --> 00:25:05.820
federal funding, which
is $975 per refugee.

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:08.670
And in green is what the
family is expected to pay.

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:10.920
As you can see for the
first three to six months,

00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:12.330
the resettlement agency, and again,

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:14.760
Iris is a much more
resourced resettlement agency

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:16.644
than anywhere else in the United States.

00:25:16.644 --> 00:25:19.470
Using a mix of federal funding
and welcome money as well

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:23.040
as donations to the agency
would support the family.

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:25.230
However, the family would
immediately be expected

00:25:25.230 --> 00:25:27.780
to pay half of the TANF
benefit towards rent

00:25:27.780 --> 00:25:29.970
while the agency would cover the rest.

00:25:29.970 --> 00:25:31.710
But when the assistance was up, right,

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:33.540
when the agency was no
longer supporting them,

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:35.873
so between that three and six months,

00:25:35.873 --> 00:25:37.657
TANF cash alone, right?

00:25:37.657 --> 00:25:39.450
$803 versus a thousand dollars

00:25:39.450 --> 00:25:42.120
for rent was insufficient to cover it.

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:44.730
And so there was no
discussion in this family as

00:25:44.730 --> 00:25:47.820
to who had to find a job, as
a household's breadwinner,

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:50.520
this was Mejed's responsibility.

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:52.470
And this brings us to the
issue of state structured

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:55.710
human capital tied to labor market entry.

00:25:55.710 --> 00:25:57.810
Mejed lacked an effective social network.

00:25:57.810 --> 00:26:00.750
His friends or refugees who
were just as poor as he was.

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:02.400
However, through a contact at the mosque,

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:04.950
he was referred to an
Egyptian pizza store owner,

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:06.780
he helped in the kitchen.

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:09.090
Mejed earn minimum wage at this job.

00:26:09.090 --> 00:26:10.290
Due to the nature of the job,

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:12.690
however, he had no
control over his shifts,

00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:15.431
which is just the nature of
shift work in the United States.

00:26:15.431 --> 00:26:17.520
Though he tried to attend English classes,

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:19.560
his work schedule was unpredictable.

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:20.880
He also did not have the time

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:23.190
or energy to study after his shift.

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:24.960
Low income work in the United States

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:26.850
is characterized by precarity,

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:28.860
with unpredictable work
schedules and a lack

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:31.380
of a living wage as well as de-skilling.

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:33.300
In Syria, the men in this sample,

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:35.430
as I said before, were
carpenters, electricians,

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:38.070
contractors, chefs and restaurant owners.

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:39.630
When they met each other
in the United States,

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:41.760
they introduced themselves
first by their family name

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:44.490
but then by their man or
their their occupations.

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:46.980
In the United States,
most did low wage jobs.

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:49.410
There were dishwashers,
janitors, gas station attendants

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:52.110
where coworkers did not speak
English by design, right?

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:53.520
Because they didn't speak English.

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:54.990
And where they did not have the energy

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:58.200
or time to attend English
classes after work.

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.960
As one man put it, "I want
to work but I'm new here.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:03.480
We just got here, we
don't understand anything

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:05.790
but you wanna push me,
'tedfeshooni' to work.

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:07.320
So, we work whatever, 'hayallah.'

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:09.330
Most work in restaurants, doing whatever,

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:11.130
everyone is working against their will.

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:12.150
No one is happy.

00:27:12.150 --> 00:27:13.290
In other countries they allow us

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:14.550
to study, to get assistance.

00:27:14.550 --> 00:27:16.200
They say you're not allowed to work

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:18.147
so you can learn, not here."

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:21.750
Meanwhile, after the
kids enrolled in school,

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:23.190
Rojette and the other women began

00:27:23.190 --> 00:27:25.470
to attend English classes in the morning.

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:26.910
By staying out of the labor market,

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:30.330
the women had the opportunity
to build new human capital,

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:32.340
the English they were
learning and enabled the women

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:35.250
to do a series of tasks that
their husbands could not.

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:37.050
They could communicate
with service providers

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:38.820
and resettlement agency staff.

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:40.890
They could advocate for
their children in school

00:27:40.890 --> 00:27:42.510
and they were building
for the citizenship exam

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:44.580
that would take that
they would be eligible

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:46.650
to take in five years time.

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:49.290
In addition, most of
the women within a year

00:27:49.290 --> 00:27:50.785
after the resettlement took on some kind

00:27:50.785 --> 00:27:54.420
of income earning task to
support their husbands.

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:56.430
The woman utilizes skills that they have.

00:27:56.430 --> 00:27:59.550
For example, Rojette crocheted
baby clothes and flower pins.

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:02.007
And 26 year old Reda,
whose husband had once said

00:28:02.007 --> 00:28:05.160
to me with finality, "Our
wives don't work," did work.

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:06.172
She prepared sweets,

00:28:06.172 --> 00:28:07.672
(Heba speaking foreign language)

00:28:07.672 --> 00:28:10.140
that her husband sold to other co-ethnics.

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:12.344
So, in this way the women
reimagined their existing

00:28:12.344 --> 00:28:15.870
human capital for new
labor market returns.

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:18.788
However, as I learned when
I began a citizenship clinic

00:28:18.788 --> 00:28:21.150
with the group that earlier this year,

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:22.920
even for the women, the
gains were short lived.

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:25.020
Actually earlier last year,
the gains were short lived.

00:28:25.020 --> 00:28:26.340
None of the activities,

00:28:26.340 --> 00:28:29.550
because they were not
recognized via a pay stub,

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:31.590
were seen by the Department
of Social Services

00:28:31.590 --> 00:28:33.030
as qualified work, right?

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:34.590
Because you have to have qualified work,

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:36.750
which means you have to show a pay stub.

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:39.480
The women could not receive
the limited federal care,

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:41.280
childcare subsidy, federal, sorry,

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:43.620
childcare subsidies that
were available to them

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:44.573
because they do not have the pay stubs,

00:28:44.573 --> 00:28:46.380
because they were not seen as working

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:48.030
from the eyes of the state.

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:50.340
Anna's family settled
in, babies were born.

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:52.080
Sarah joked Donald Trump is trying

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:53.460
to keep Syrians out of the country.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:56.610
But look at us, we're
making more Syrians here.

00:28:56.610 --> 00:28:58.260
With nowhere to leave their children,

00:28:58.260 --> 00:29:01.170
the women dropped out of English classes.

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:03.218
Also, it's important to note that women

00:29:03.218 --> 00:29:05.340
were also hindered in
their pursuit of language

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:07.500
as a result of anti-Muslim racism.

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:09.210
On your screen is a hateful message sent

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:11.250
to the local mosque here in 2016,

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:14.160
was sent to mosques
all around the country.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:16.290
As hate crimes against Muslims spiked,

00:29:16.290 --> 00:29:18.870
including incidents against
people in this study,

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:21.720
they shied away from being
outdoors on their own.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:23.970
Reda, for instance, stopped
going to English classes

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:26.280
altogether after her family was attacked.

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:28.470
And Rojette also stopped
going for a period of time

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:30.690
after Reda's family was attacked.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:31.980
Five years after their arrival,

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:33.900
none of the men and few of the women had

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:36.870
enough English to qualify
for the citizenship exam.

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:39.060
A crucial barrier to
inclusion and protection

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:41.640
against policies like the
travel ban that reduce

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:44.523
their feelings of security
in the United States.

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:48.330
So, to sum up, the
self-sufficiency imperative

00:29:48.330 --> 00:29:51.000
interacted with men and
women's division of labor

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:52.860
to result in men entering the labor market

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:54.660
within months of their
arrival, which reduced

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:57.300
their ability to rebuild
their human capital.

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:59.880
Meanwhile, women staying
out of the labor market

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:01.650
because their husbands
entered in their stead

00:30:01.650 --> 00:30:03.990
were able to begin to learn more English

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:06.300
and reimagine their skills in new ways.

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:07.740
But five years after their arrival,

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:10.770
they too did not have much
by way of language capital.

00:30:10.770 --> 00:30:12.780
My findings linked to national data,

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:14.310
which shows that though refugees enter

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:16.590
the labor market quickly
in the United States

00:30:16.590 --> 00:30:18.660
and are employed at higher
rates than non immigrants,

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:20.040
they tend to have lower incomes

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:22.380
than the native born and other immigrants.

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:26.010
And this gap is increasing
for new cohorts.

00:30:26.010 --> 00:30:28.440
Importantly, 20 years in over half

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:30.120
of refugees admitted to the United States

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:32.940
continue to have low English proficiency.

00:30:32.940 --> 00:30:34.920
So, the experience of refugee resettlement

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:36.720
in the United States is an integration

00:30:36.720 --> 00:30:39.750
into American poverty,
which by design is populated

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:42.363
by people of color and
marked by stagnation.

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:46.650
Okay, so now that I've given you the case

00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:49.680
of the United States, let's
turn to the case of Canada.

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:51.816
So, a drive of 500
miles north of New Haven

00:30:51.816 --> 00:30:54.420
takes us to Toronto, where Venya,

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:56.310
a relative of one of the
families in New Haven,

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:58.470
lived with her husband and four children.

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:00.660
Venya has a laugh that
can be heard ringing down

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:02.580
the hallway of her apartment building.

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:05.430
Her husband Bassim is a
good deal older than her

00:31:05.430 --> 00:31:08.820
and like mentioned he owned
his own restaurant in Homs.

00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:10.350
The couple has three children,

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:12.330
including a son who
requires a hearing aid,

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:16.410
and was placed in a school
the for Canada, excuse me,

00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:17.970
something that Venya was really ecstatic

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:20.320
about 'cause he was gonna
get proper education.

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:23.670
When Bassim and Venya arrived, COSTY,

00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:26.043
an organization that was
managing resettlement on behalf

00:31:26.043 --> 00:31:29.250
of the Canadian government,
housed them in a hotel.

00:31:29.250 --> 00:31:31.680
They would be allowed to
choose their own residence.

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:33.750
They chose to move to Mississauga, an area

00:31:33.750 --> 00:31:35.940
with a large Arab community
where a market around

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:38.400
the corner sells the
latest in Islamic fashion

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:41.310
and transplants the customer
to an old town soul.

00:31:41.310 --> 00:31:44.310
Two other families in my study
chose to move to Kitchener

00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:47.070
and Hamilton with a group
of other families starting

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:49.530
a Syrian community out of
the arrivals in Toronto,

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:52.170
people that they did not previously know.

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:54.900
The Canadian government gave
families a startup allowance

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:58.140
of $5,000 to cover first
and last month's rent.

00:31:58.140 --> 00:31:59.880
The government also provided new arrivals

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:02.520
with standard issue, new
furniture for their home.

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:04.710
For the families resettled
through private sponsors,

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:06.450
the sponsors would find family a home,

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:08.130
purchase it and furnish it for them.

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:10.050
So, at a similar level. Tournament

00:32:10.050 --> 00:32:13.387
This family received a cash
assistance of $1,600 a month,

00:32:13.387 --> 00:32:17.400
$800 per parent in addition
to the Canadian child benefit,

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:20.430
which is approximately
$450 per school age child

00:32:20.430 --> 00:32:21.835
and available to everyone who

00:32:21.835 --> 00:32:25.560
is low income and documented in Canada.

00:32:25.560 --> 00:32:27.381
After the first year,
the families who still

00:32:27.381 --> 00:32:30.210
need assistance would
receive Canadian welfare,

00:32:30.210 --> 00:32:31.700
which is a variable for
low income Canadians,

00:32:31.700 --> 00:32:33.690
which was a few hundred dollars less

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:35.250
than the refugee cash assistance,

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:38.040
but still supported the
needs of these families.

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:40.500
The Canadian government, as
is clear on their website,

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:41.736
anticipated this transition

00:32:41.736 --> 00:32:43.950
from refugee assistance to welfare.

00:32:43.950 --> 00:32:46.830
So, they write when income
support from the government

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:49.560
or private sponsors ends after 12 months,

00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:51.480
in most cases it is a normal occurrence

00:32:51.480 --> 00:32:53.640
for some refugees transition to provincial

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:56.670
or territorial social assistance support.

00:32:56.670 --> 00:32:59.460
So, Venya and Bassim's rent
for a three bedroom apartment

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:02.130
in this high rise was $1,400 a month,

00:33:02.130 --> 00:33:04.920
which they would cover and
exceed with this assistance.

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:06.990
So, the families did not
need to follow the timeline

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:08.430
of families in the United States.

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:10.680
They could wait to enter the labor market.

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:12.420
In fact, they were incentivized to do so

00:33:12.420 --> 00:33:14.490
because they would lose
the refugee cash assistance

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:16.390
if they had income from other sources.

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:19.620
This differentially structured
their human capital, right?

00:33:19.620 --> 00:33:21.270
Differently structured
their human capital as

00:33:21.270 --> 00:33:24.420
it allowed them to learn
English and gain skills.

00:33:24.420 --> 00:33:26.460
Unlike in the United States
where men had to enter

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:29.110
the labor market early
and women were the ones

00:33:29.110 --> 00:33:31.230
who could learn English, in Canada,

00:33:31.230 --> 00:33:34.350
the opportunity was
available to both partners.

00:33:34.350 --> 00:33:36.510
And so each morning the Bassim and Venya

00:33:36.510 --> 00:33:39.270
attended English classes a
short walk from their home.

00:33:39.270 --> 00:33:42.120
After a year of lessons,
their English remained halting

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:44.610
but enabled them to have
telephone conversations,

00:33:44.610 --> 00:33:47.460
something that is really hard
to do in a foreign language

00:33:47.460 --> 00:33:49.110
and something that
neither the men or women

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:51.750
could accomplish in the
United States at that time.

00:33:51.750 --> 00:33:53.640
And while there was
variation in the sample as

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:55.290
to who attended regularly,

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:57.390
everyone reported that
English classes as well as

00:33:57.390 --> 00:33:59.610
opportunities for education like courses

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:00.870
through which they could gain skills

00:34:00.870 --> 00:34:03.753
or earn diplomas were free
and readily available.

00:34:04.950 --> 00:34:06.704
The one caveat I will
say is that childcare

00:34:06.704 --> 00:34:08.130
was a little bit spotty.

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:09.300
So, while in a lot of places,

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:11.940
childcare was also
included, in some cases,

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:14.370
like for a family that was in Kitchener,

00:34:14.370 --> 00:34:17.610
it was harder to access
government sponsored childcare.

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:19.200
However, the system was uneven

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:21.030
in recognizing existing skills.

00:34:21.030 --> 00:34:22.380
While a blacksmith in the study

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:24.120
could find work immediately
as a blacksmith,

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:27.060
for instance, a woman
who was a nurse in Syria

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:29.250
and who had confronted the war as a nurse

00:34:29.250 --> 00:34:31.170
could not gain equivalency, particularly

00:34:31.170 --> 00:34:33.810
as she lost her paperwork
fleeing from the war.

00:34:33.810 --> 00:34:35.550
She was told she'd need
at least five years

00:34:35.550 --> 00:34:38.130
of schooling to become a nurse in Canada.

00:34:38.130 --> 00:34:40.040
An electrician in the study was hired in

00:34:40.040 --> 00:34:43.860
an apprenticeship based on
his existing credentials,

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:46.530
but he was told he would
need to pass an exam

00:34:46.530 --> 00:34:49.020
to become fully licensed in the country.

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:51.690
All three however, and
others in the study benefited

00:34:51.690 --> 00:34:53.940
from publicly available in free education.

00:34:53.940 --> 00:34:57.120
The blacksmith to courses on
becoming a forklift operator,

00:34:57.120 --> 00:34:59.864
trying to find different things
because he had a bad back.

00:34:59.864 --> 00:35:03.902
The electrician was considering
earning a certificate

00:35:03.902 --> 00:35:06.570
in engineering since he
had to do an exam anyway,

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:09.630
so he figured he'd get the
next stage of his education

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:11.700
and the nurse took classes
in community counseling

00:35:11.700 --> 00:35:12.840
though she wasn't sure that she could

00:35:12.840 --> 00:35:14.290
make a full career out of it.

00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:18.020
The existence of these
classes benefited others too.

00:35:18.020 --> 00:35:20.467
Venya said in her usual a jovial demeanor,

00:35:20.467 --> 00:35:22.168
"Venya that cooks and cleans is over."

00:35:22.168 --> 00:35:23.001
(Heba speaking foreign language)

00:35:23.001 --> 00:35:25.590
She was gonna enroll in a
class to learn to do hair

00:35:25.590 --> 00:35:27.540
and Yasin, who was Almir
the blacksmith's wife

00:35:27.540 --> 00:35:31.140
took classes in food prep and
began to work at a restaurant.

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:33.060
So, it's important to
note that in Canada, too,

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:35.010
hate crimes were high against Muslims.

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:37.650
Almost as high as they
were in the United States.

00:35:37.650 --> 00:35:39.635
However, the families
in my study in a sense

00:35:39.635 --> 00:35:42.600
felt insulated from it, and even though

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:45.630
ethnography is not a good
measure of incidents,

00:35:45.630 --> 00:35:47.430
this feeling of security says something

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:48.690
'cause it was not the case

00:35:48.690 --> 00:35:51.030
in the United States or in Germany.

00:35:51.030 --> 00:35:52.992
One explanation is that
the Syrians in Canada,

00:35:52.992 --> 00:35:55.770
unlike the US and Germany
could choose where they lived.

00:35:55.770 --> 00:35:56.670
They had free mobility.

00:35:56.670 --> 00:35:58.440
So, a lot of them live by co-ethnics,

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:01.726
which facilitated also their
economic opportunities.

00:36:01.726 --> 00:36:04.350
They could also choose to
live in a cosmopolitan place

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:07.200
like Toronto, which has other immigrants.

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:10.020
However, I also think that
what cannot be discounted

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:12.150
is Canada's active promotion of a policy

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:14.340
of multiculturalism by law,

00:36:14.340 --> 00:36:16.320
which as Irene Bloemraad describes,

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:20.040
means that the state is
focused on investing in people

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:21.870
through the aforementioned
language courses

00:36:21.870 --> 00:36:23.220
and educational opportunities,

00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:25.020
but also through things
like radio programming

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:28.194
in their language and
support for co-ethnic groups.

00:36:28.194 --> 00:36:30.900
And so this, and these
are designed specifically

00:36:30.900 --> 00:36:33.300
by the state to generate
the sense of belonging.

00:36:35.070 --> 00:36:38.070
So, in sum, through investment
the Canadian government

00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:41.250
supported refugees
expression of human capital

00:36:41.250 --> 00:36:44.640
and gaining of new human capital
through language learning.

00:36:44.640 --> 00:36:46.650
Though their recognition is inconsistent.

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:48.990
We don't see as a result
as strong of a divergence

00:36:48.990 --> 00:36:52.440
as we see in the case of
the United States by gender.

00:36:52.440 --> 00:36:55.470
A Syrian outcomes report done
in Canada found that among

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:57.740
government sponsored refugees
who arrived in Canada

00:36:57.740 --> 00:37:02.520
in 2015, 43% had found
stable employment by 2018.

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:04.440
So, that's a relatively low number

00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:06.450
as compared to the United States.

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:09.660
However, this has a here a
clear human capital trade off

00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:12.180
because the same study shows that 7 in 10

00:37:12.180 --> 00:37:15.390
of these people attended
English classes regularly,

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:18.150
over 80% reported feeling
that they could go shopping,

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:20.310
go to their doctor's office and interact

00:37:20.310 --> 00:37:23.550
in social situations entirely in English.

00:37:23.550 --> 00:37:26.130
And studies show that
15 years after arrival,

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:29.550
refugee income in Canada and
labor market participation

00:37:29.550 --> 00:37:31.590
looks pretty similar to
that of other immigrants

00:37:31.590 --> 00:37:34.680
and Canadians and their English
tends to be more fluent.

00:37:34.680 --> 00:37:36.180
So, in some of the Canadian system,

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:38.280
by creating opportunities
for the expression

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:40.710
of existing human capital and the creation

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:43.309
of new human capital
creates a stronger sense of

00:37:43.309 --> 00:37:46.710
sort of stability and
belonging that we don't see

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:49.590
in the counterparts in the United States.

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:52.230
Okay, so finally, what
about the case of Germany?

00:37:52.230 --> 00:37:54.480
So, arrival in the
German case of asylum is

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:57.870
much more dangerous than in
the case of resettlement.

00:37:57.870 --> 00:37:59.940
So, Iman, along with our husband Amin

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:01.230
and their three children arrived

00:38:01.230 --> 00:38:03.660
in Germany in the summer of 2015.

00:38:03.660 --> 00:38:05.790
Amin, who I had mentioned
earlier in the talk,

00:38:05.790 --> 00:38:08.430
was a long haul truck driver in Damascus

00:38:08.430 --> 00:38:10.110
where he was in prison due to tipping off

00:38:10.110 --> 00:38:12.810
the resistance on a
delivery of ammunition.

00:38:12.810 --> 00:38:15.750
After he was released from
prison by the intervention

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:18.030
of his boss, the family fled the country.

00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:19.620
They first fled to Turkey

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:22.170
and then a year later
got on a raft to Greece.

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:24.120
After an 18 day trek through Europe,

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:26.460
including three days of
detainment in Hungary,

00:38:26.460 --> 00:38:28.620
which Iman describes as
the worst days in her life,

00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:30.817
including the war, they
arrived in Germany,

00:38:30.817 --> 00:38:34.080
$400 in debt to someone
they'd met on the way.

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:35.580
They could not afford to travel further

00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:37.650
to their intended Sweden.

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:39.240
After a welcoming arrival in Germany

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:40.710
that brought you Iman to tears,

00:38:40.710 --> 00:38:42.420
they spent two days in a police camp

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:45.060
and then a month in a
refugee camp in Lysik,

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:46.290
at which point they secured their

00:38:46.290 --> 00:38:48.420
three year temporary residency.

00:38:48.420 --> 00:38:49.560
This was a quick turnaround.

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:51.660
Typically the process
takes closer to a year

00:38:51.660 --> 00:38:53.660
to get the residency card if not longer.

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:56.010
Iman and Amin received assistance

00:38:56.010 --> 00:38:58.080
from the German government
through the job center

00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:01.770
where all unemployment assistance
is distributed in Germany.

00:39:01.770 --> 00:39:04.830
Their rent in Dusseldorf was
covered by the job center

00:39:04.830 --> 00:39:06.540
and they received a
monthly amount that covered

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:08.468
their basic expenditures
and allowed for them

00:39:08.468 --> 00:39:13.020
to send 50 euros back home
every once in a while.

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:15.390
Like in Canada, refugees language learning

00:39:15.390 --> 00:39:18.660
is the first issue of concern
for the German government.

00:39:18.660 --> 00:39:21.930
However, unlike in Ontario,
Syrian refugees are required

00:39:21.930 --> 00:39:25.020
to attend German classes
under penalty of reducing

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:27.900
their assistance or
canceling their assistance.

00:39:27.900 --> 00:39:30.720
As a result, Amin and
Iman enrolled in classes.

00:39:30.720 --> 00:39:32.550
In order to secure work in Germany,

00:39:32.550 --> 00:39:33.540
they would have to accomplish

00:39:33.540 --> 00:39:35.790
at least the level B one of German.

00:39:35.790 --> 00:39:37.773
And so this meant
succeeding in four courses,

00:39:37.773 --> 00:39:41.130
A levels, three A levels and
the first of your B level,

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:42.660
which would take a minimum of a year,

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:44.711
but more realistically two, particularly

00:39:44.711 --> 00:39:46.890
for people who were illiterate in Arabic

00:39:46.890 --> 00:39:50.760
or who were in this you
know, semi credential group.

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:53.220
The classes also taught
basics about German culture

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:55.050
and laws, which people have written about.

00:39:55.050 --> 00:39:57.240
And you know, they tend
to take a racialized view

00:39:57.240 --> 00:39:58.530
of the immigrant who's arriving.

00:39:58.530 --> 00:40:01.140
But we could talk about
that in the Q and A.

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:04.110
But then if Amin wanted to
be a truck driver in Germany

00:40:04.110 --> 00:40:05.340
he would need to do training through

00:40:05.340 --> 00:40:06.627
an apprenticeship system called an

00:40:06.627 --> 00:40:07.860
(Heba speaking foreign language)

00:40:07.860 --> 00:40:10.080
for an additional three years.

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:12.480
His experience as a
truck driver and the fact

00:40:12.480 --> 00:40:14.370
that he had gotten arrested
in tortured in prison

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:17.370
as a result of this
occupation was irrelevant.

00:40:17.370 --> 00:40:19.110
It wasn't seen by the German state

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:21.120
as qualifying Amin to work.

00:40:21.120 --> 00:40:22.890
In order to find work in Germany,

00:40:22.890 --> 00:40:25.620
he would have to abide by
the same rules as Germans.

00:40:25.620 --> 00:40:27.730
This made Syrian refugees feel inadequate.

00:40:27.730 --> 00:40:30.937
Azodae, a 31 year old former mechanic said

00:40:30.937 --> 00:40:32.820
"The Germans see themselves here,

00:40:32.820 --> 00:40:35.370
if you wanna be with us, you
have to catch up with us.

00:40:35.370 --> 00:40:37.740
When you get here it's
like you're a newborn."

00:40:37.740 --> 00:40:39.750
Another respondent was more explicit,

00:40:39.750 --> 00:40:41.790
calling the system racist and explaining

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:43.500
there's no recognition that I'm coming

00:40:43.500 --> 00:40:45.780
from Syria within 11th grade education

00:40:45.780 --> 00:40:48.390
and that I wanna continue
to finish my education.

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:50.730
Why can't I just continue on? It's racist.

00:40:50.730 --> 00:40:52.080
We left war and we came here

00:40:52.080 --> 00:40:53.853
to be met by this racist system.

00:40:54.690 --> 00:40:56.340
For some the lack of recognition

00:40:56.340 --> 00:40:58.620
of their professional
history as human capital

00:40:58.620 --> 00:41:01.110
that matters for the German
labor market regarded

00:41:01.110 --> 00:41:04.170
on their giving up on an
attempt to secure equivalency.

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:06.180
So, in Bavaria, BMW hired people through

00:41:06.180 --> 00:41:08.370
these temp agencies and a lot of people,

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:09.840
despite the fact that
you're not really earning

00:41:09.840 --> 00:41:11.160
the credential, a lot of
people just went through

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:13.650
these temp agencies that
actually were exploitative

00:41:13.650 --> 00:41:16.263
and took a little bit off
every euro they earned.

00:41:17.220 --> 00:41:19.350
But for everybody, a main driver to enter

00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:21.863
the German economy was a fear
that the German government

00:41:21.863 --> 00:41:24.150
would not renew their
three year residencies

00:41:24.150 --> 00:41:25.950
unless Syrians proved to be productive.

00:41:25.950 --> 00:41:27.120
And the German government
actually ended up

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:28.770
transitioning to one year residencies,

00:41:28.770 --> 00:41:31.740
which let people feeling
very, very worried.

00:41:31.740 --> 00:41:34.290
They trusted Anglo Merkel but they worried

00:41:34.290 --> 00:41:35.400
about what would come after her,

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:38.220
which we're, you know,
beginning to see now.

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:40.080
So, for women by contrast,

00:41:40.080 --> 00:41:41.550
the credentialization requirement

00:41:41.550 --> 00:41:43.590
cleared a pathway to the labor market.

00:41:43.590 --> 00:41:45.120
Yarrah, who had married her husband,

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:46.830
a Palestinian refugee in Syria when

00:41:46.830 --> 00:41:49.980
she was 15 years old,
said that she would not

00:41:49.980 --> 00:41:52.627
have continued education
or worked in Syria.

00:41:52.627 --> 00:41:54.577
"I dropped out of school
young," she told me.

00:41:54.577 --> 00:41:55.980
"I wasn't a particularly good student

00:41:55.980 --> 00:41:57.240
and I got married young.

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:58.410
In Syria, I would've taken care

00:41:58.410 --> 00:42:00.540
of my house and our kids."

00:42:00.540 --> 00:42:01.470
In Germany, however,

00:42:01.470 --> 00:42:03.840
she wanted to become a dental hygienist.

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:05.190
Three years after her arrival,

00:42:05.190 --> 00:42:07.350
she was finishing up her
B one and in the process

00:42:07.350 --> 00:42:09.240
of applying for an
(Heba speaking German)

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:11.850
Similarly, Madwa who
arrived with her husband

00:42:11.850 --> 00:42:14.460
and one child and who was
pregnant with a second,

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:15.780
knew that she would attend.

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:17.460
She would also enter the labor market.

00:42:17.460 --> 00:42:19.792
She said, of course here
you need two incomes.

00:42:19.792 --> 00:42:21.879
Shrugging when I asked
her if she'd discussed it

00:42:21.879 --> 00:42:24.360
with her husband, she said,
"It's an obvious thing,"

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:27.540
like for her it was expected
that she would enter.

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:29.970
But here too, it's important
to highlight the role

00:42:29.970 --> 00:42:33.300
of racism and shaping the
experiences of new arrivals.

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:35.490
The image on your screen
is of hateful messages

00:42:35.490 --> 00:42:38.400
one woman received in her
mailbox from a neighbor.

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:40.650
Not only did new arrivals
see the policies as

00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:43.260
the German state as exclusionary
or as one respondent

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:45.780
put it, a German system
that works for Germans.

00:42:45.780 --> 00:42:47.910
But also they felt that they were expected

00:42:47.910 --> 00:42:50.280
to relinquish parts of their
identities to better fit

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:53.940
German life culture or
leading culture in Germany.

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:56.700
In Germany where Turks are
substantial immigrant minority,

00:42:56.700 --> 00:42:59.580
estimated to be about 5%
of the German population,

00:42:59.580 --> 00:43:01.920
the Syrians are racialized
as Muslim in relation

00:43:01.920 --> 00:43:05.550
to this group that is perceived
to not want assimilate.

00:43:05.550 --> 00:43:08.760
Particularly meaningful for the
expression of human capital,

00:43:08.760 --> 00:43:10.320
women were turned away from a job

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:12.330
because they wear the hijab.

00:43:12.330 --> 00:43:14.700
For instance, a 22 year old woman was told

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:16.470
by a government employee at the job center

00:43:16.470 --> 00:43:18.150
that she could not work in a daycare

00:43:18.150 --> 00:43:20.010
while wearing her hijab
because it would send

00:43:20.010 --> 00:43:22.050
the wrong message to the children, right?

00:43:22.050 --> 00:43:24.000
So, this racialization, this gendered

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:26.010
and discrimination exists alongside

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:28.230
is integral to the system.

00:43:28.230 --> 00:43:32.490
So, in sum, in Germany, the
system of credentialization

00:43:32.490 --> 00:43:33.900
ignored labor market histories

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:36.210
and lengthened pathways
to the labor market.

00:43:36.210 --> 00:43:38.040
There was a strong divergence by gender

00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:40.364
and that people with long
labor market histories,

00:43:40.364 --> 00:43:43.770
often men were disadvantaged
by the non recognition

00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:45.630
and people without labor market histories

00:43:45.630 --> 00:43:48.030
who were often women but also young people

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:50.550
were advantaged by investment
in credentialization.

00:43:50.550 --> 00:43:53.490
However, there important
caveats given women's

00:43:53.490 --> 00:43:56.103
unique experience of
racism in this system.

00:43:57.450 --> 00:43:58.950
Three years into their asylum,

00:43:58.950 --> 00:44:01.920
72% of Syrians remained
unemployed in Germany,

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:05.640
according to you know,
some data gathered in 2019,

00:44:05.640 --> 00:44:08.310
despite a desperate need for
them in the German labor market

00:44:08.310 --> 00:44:11.213
where unemployment at the
time was hovering at just 4%.

00:44:12.180 --> 00:44:13.980
The erasure of skills
and abilities through

00:44:13.980 --> 00:44:16.137
non recognition is detrimental to asylies

00:44:16.137 --> 00:44:18.540
who have more to offer and
who experienced financial,

00:44:18.540 --> 00:44:21.063
political and emotional
injury by not being able

00:44:21.063 --> 00:44:23.520
to participate in the labor market.

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:25.173
It also injures the German state

00:44:25.173 --> 00:44:27.030
that is looking for their contributions.

00:44:27.030 --> 00:44:28.140
So, according to a lot of people,

00:44:28.140 --> 00:44:29.730
the reason the Germans admitted, you know,

00:44:29.730 --> 00:44:30.750
half a million Syrians is

00:44:30.750 --> 00:44:32.913
because they wanted to use their labor.

00:44:34.110 --> 00:44:37.170
The case emphasizes that in
addition to investment states

00:44:37.170 --> 00:44:38.790
make through incorporation policies,

00:44:38.790 --> 00:44:42.210
recognition is key to expressing
existing human capital.

00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:44.970
In short, the presence of a
German system for Germans leaves

00:44:44.970 --> 00:44:48.243
refugees feeling marginalized
economically and otherwise.

00:44:49.320 --> 00:44:50.760
So, now that I presented my data,

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:53.520
I conclude by returning
to my main argument.

00:44:53.520 --> 00:44:55.947
The global ethnography
centers the experiences of men

00:44:55.947 --> 00:44:59.340
and women who come from similar
socioeconomic backgrounds in

00:44:59.340 --> 00:45:02.430
their initial years and three
countries of permanent refuge.

00:45:02.430 --> 00:45:05.190
It advances a theory of state
structured human capital

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:07.210
by showing that human
capital often conceptualizes

00:45:07.210 --> 00:45:09.870
to determinate of refugee outcomes

00:45:09.870 --> 00:45:12.150
is a product of national
incorporation policy.

00:45:12.150 --> 00:45:13.770
And specifically whether
countries are willing

00:45:13.770 --> 00:45:16.920
to invest in and recognize
refugee potential.

00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:19.770
In Germany, a diminished
ability to use human capital is

00:45:19.770 --> 00:45:21.330
an outcome of a lack of recognition

00:45:21.330 --> 00:45:23.940
of immigrant histories as human capital.

00:45:23.940 --> 00:45:24.870
In the United States,

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:27.330
a diminished ability to
use preexisting skills in

00:45:27.330 --> 00:45:30.120
the labor market is an outcome
of a lack of investment

00:45:30.120 --> 00:45:32.130
in newly arrived refugees
so that they can use

00:45:32.130 --> 00:45:33.990
their skills and capacities productively

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:36.600
in the United States
economy and in Canada,

00:45:36.600 --> 00:45:40.260
a mixture of imperfect re-investment
and recognition results

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:43.380
in an ability to use and
create new human capital.

00:45:43.380 --> 00:45:45.120
These processes, as I've described before,

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:46.380
are gendered and racialized,

00:45:46.380 --> 00:45:48.830
as are the welfare systems
that they derive from.

00:45:49.980 --> 00:45:52.140
And so what are the
implications for this project?

00:45:52.140 --> 00:45:53.690
I'm gonna leave you with three.

00:45:54.630 --> 00:45:56.382
First, in accounts of refugee crises

00:45:56.382 --> 00:45:58.080
and in international laws,

00:45:58.080 --> 00:46:00.570
refugees are cast as
victims in receiving states

00:46:00.570 --> 00:46:02.070
that offer these durable solutions

00:46:02.070 --> 00:46:04.440
are often cast as saviors.

00:46:04.440 --> 00:46:06.870
Implicit to this is the
racist idea that life

00:46:06.870 --> 00:46:09.300
in the west is necessarily a privilege

00:46:09.300 --> 00:46:10.920
and therefore countries are accomplishing

00:46:10.920 --> 00:46:14.670
their humanitarian commitment
simply by admitting refugees.

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:18.720
But admission into American
poverty is no one's salvation.

00:46:18.720 --> 00:46:20.850
Indeed, wealthy countries
that receive refugees,

00:46:20.850 --> 00:46:23.160
including the United
States, Canada, and Germany,

00:46:23.160 --> 00:46:25.380
are complicit in the colonial legacies,

00:46:25.380 --> 00:46:28.140
foreign military interventions
and environmental degradation

00:46:28.140 --> 00:46:30.270
that create the very
conditions from which refugees

00:46:30.270 --> 00:46:32.430
are fleeing and within their borders,

00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:34.110
they too are capable of violence,

00:46:34.110 --> 00:46:37.260
the violence of minoritizing,
of denying living wages,

00:46:37.260 --> 00:46:40.050
of a non recognition of people's humanity.

00:46:40.050 --> 00:46:42.690
So, this project captures how
countries equip new arrivals

00:46:42.690 --> 00:46:45.090
for their pursuit of a
good stable life through

00:46:45.090 --> 00:46:46.890
the very same systems
that have long shaped

00:46:46.890 --> 00:46:49.260
the contours of national belonging.

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:52.110
Second and following,
observing how the human capital

00:46:52.110 --> 00:46:54.840
of those seeking refuges
structured reveals the ways

00:46:54.840 --> 00:46:57.390
in which it matters well
beyond the labor market.

00:46:57.390 --> 00:46:59.970
Language acquisition is
necessary for everything,

00:46:59.970 --> 00:47:01.504
for making friends to securing citizenship

00:47:01.504 --> 00:47:04.020
in all three of these countries.

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:06.120
And the ability to express human potential

00:47:06.120 --> 00:47:07.800
is emotionally important.

00:47:07.800 --> 00:47:09.930
How would any of us feel
if all we have accomplished

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:11.490
and built professionally suddenly became

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:14.460
completely meaningless
to everybody around us?

00:47:14.460 --> 00:47:16.733
Finally, the study has
implications well beyond

00:47:16.733 --> 00:47:19.380
the case of refugees or even immigrants

00:47:19.380 --> 00:47:21.930
through a design that follows
the simultaneous experience

00:47:21.930 --> 00:47:24.000
of people seeking refuge who are strangers

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:26.280
to these systems of social welfare.

00:47:26.280 --> 00:47:28.380
We raise a mirror to
the systems themselves

00:47:28.380 --> 00:47:31.080
and how they shape
inequality more broadly.

00:47:31.080 --> 00:47:33.270
The national incorporation
policies that structure

00:47:33.270 --> 00:47:35.850
the human capital refugees
are the same systems

00:47:35.850 --> 00:47:38.220
which low income people
have traversed across

00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:41.010
these three countries for
decades, right, if not centuries,

00:47:41.010 --> 00:47:44.580
well before this cohort of
Syrian refugees arrived.

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:47.663
Thank you.
(crowd applauding)

