00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:46.200
But, in every time I
give a talk in Germany,

00:52:46.200 --> 00:52:48.360
and this is a roundabout
way to answer your question,

00:52:48.360 --> 00:52:51.330
but every time I give a talk in Europe,

00:52:51.330 --> 00:52:55.530
somebody asks me a bonkers
question about, you know,

00:52:55.530 --> 00:52:59.370
that assumes that race doesn't
exist, every single time.

00:52:59.370 --> 00:53:01.798
You know, maybe there's
like very few exceptions.

00:53:01.798 --> 00:53:04.327
I was actually one argument he said,

00:53:04.327 --> 00:53:05.910
"Well, you can't just make anything up.

00:53:05.910 --> 00:53:09.000
Syrians and Italians are
the same skin tone, right?"

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:10.530
This was his argument, right?

00:53:10.530 --> 00:53:11.700
And so when we think about it,

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:15.518
I think that in the United
States we address race

00:53:15.518 --> 00:53:17.970
and racism in ways, we
have a different language.

00:53:17.970 --> 00:53:20.220
Not to say that it's less
racist, I think, you know,

00:53:20.220 --> 00:53:22.020
I don't think that we
can talk about magnitude.

00:53:22.020 --> 00:53:23.910
I don't think it's a
useful conversation to have

00:53:23.910 --> 00:53:26.250
about what places are more or less racist,

00:53:26.250 --> 00:53:28.470
but I do think that we have a conversation

00:53:28.470 --> 00:53:30.510
about it here whereas in the German case,

00:53:30.510 --> 00:53:32.190
it's all talked about in terms of culture

00:53:32.190 --> 00:53:33.720
and even educated academics.

00:53:33.720 --> 00:53:35.847
Even sociologists will sit in front of me

00:53:35.847 --> 00:53:38.310
