00:55:10.770 --> 00:55:12.360
So, when I talk about structure,

00:55:12.360 --> 00:55:14.070
when I'm talking about
the reason I'm focusing on

00:55:14.070 --> 00:55:16.294
the welfare systems is to say that here

00:55:16.294 --> 00:55:19.230
is the framework in which
people have to operate.

00:55:19.230 --> 00:55:24.090
But people, the EITC, the
earned income tax credit

00:55:24.090 --> 00:55:26.430
was a huge resource that people use,

00:55:26.430 --> 00:55:27.420
the earn income tax credit,

00:55:27.420 --> 00:55:28.560
for those who don't know is

00:55:28.560 --> 00:55:30.861
a government subsidy
for low income families.

00:55:30.861 --> 00:55:32.617
And it's actually one
of the more effective,

00:55:32.617 --> 00:55:35.330
if you ask me what my two
favorite government subsidies are

00:55:35.330 --> 00:55:37.730
or what two favorite, you know,
poverty interventions are.

00:55:37.730 --> 00:55:40.560
Either earned income tax
credit and food stamps, right?

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:42.510
I mean, honestly, I mean
they're all, but like

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:44.730
it would be the first income
tax credit and food stamps.

00:55:44.730 --> 00:55:47.820
And it's because I have
seen people use that money,

00:55:47.820 --> 00:55:50.220
which comes in April as like
a little bit of a bulk money.

00:55:50.220 --> 00:55:52.320
Its, you know, for low income families

00:55:52.320 --> 00:55:54.540
with a lot of children
could be as much as $5,000

00:55:54.540 --> 00:55:57.630
and use that to invest
in a car or to use that

00:55:57.630 --> 00:55:59.550
to help them with the
down payment on a house

00:55:59.550 --> 00:56:01.650
or to use that even to
invest in a business.

00:56:01.650 --> 00:56:04.350
You know, people have also
sold property in Syria

00:56:04.350 --> 00:56:06.097
at a huge, huge discount that they had.

00:56:06.097 --> 00:56:08.490
You know, once things
calm down a little bit,

00:56:08.490 --> 00:56:09.949
were able to use that money.

00:56:09.949 --> 00:56:14.220
People, you know, people have, you know,

00:56:14.220 --> 00:56:15.450
communicated with each other,

00:56:15.450 --> 00:56:17.130
made partnerships with each other.

00:56:17.130 --> 00:56:19.936
Sometimes those fail because
you're basically like,

00:56:19.936 --> 00:56:21.680
you know, everybody's kind
of in a precarious place

00:56:21.680 --> 00:56:24.234
and so it's really hard
to start a business.

00:56:24.234 --> 00:56:25.890
But these are all different ways

00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:27.120
that people have pushed back.

00:56:27.120 --> 00:56:28.170
I also think that, you know,

00:56:28.170 --> 00:56:30.594
