to focus on government policies

01:07:42.510 --> 01:07:44.730
because I think it's misplaced criticism.

01:07:44.730 --> 01:07:48.090
Like, could NGOs do
better in their position?

01:07:48.090 --> 01:07:49.860
Could they do better to advocate?

01:07:49.860 --> 01:07:52.890
Could they recognize more
how unfair the system is?

01:07:52.890 --> 01:07:53.790
Absolutely, right?

01:07:53.790 --> 01:07:55.530
But the thing is, is that actually

01:07:55.530 --> 01:07:57.810
after I published the book,
I have been surprised,

01:07:57.810 --> 01:08:00.993
you know, Lutheran Refugee, you know,

01:08:00.993 --> 01:08:03.043
the, one of the voluntary agencies,

01:08:03.043 --> 01:08:05.250
which is a national,
runs a lot of different

01:08:05.250 --> 01:08:06.660
resettlement agencies reached out to me

01:08:06.660 --> 01:08:08.640
after the book and said,
"What can we do better?"

01:08:08.640 --> 01:08:10.890
Right, so there is I think a willingness,

01:08:10.890 --> 01:08:12.720
and I do wanna work with those people

01:08:12.720 --> 01:08:14.010
for these intermediary effects

01:08:14.010 --> 01:08:16.487
'cause this impacts people's
lives immediately, right?

01:08:16.487 --> 01:08:19.680
And so I am really keen
on supporting, you know,

01:08:19.680 --> 01:08:22.470
the work of NGOs who are tasked with this.

01:08:22.470 --> 01:08:26.820
And so I'm reticent to participate
in that kind of rhetoric.

01:08:26.820 --> 01:08:28.350
And the reason for that is because I think

01:08:28.350 --> 01:08:32.850
that the real violence
and the real crisis lies

01:08:32.850 --> 01:08:34.740
with the government just not funding this.

01:08:34.740 --> 01:08:37.230
And so the NGOs are put
in an impossible position,

01:08:37.230 --> 01:08:38.880
like, can we all do our work better?

01:08:38.880 --> 01:08:39.930
Absolutely.

01:08:39.930 --> 01:08:41.454
Is there really a way to do this well

01:08:41.454 --> 01:08:43.050
given the resources that they have?

01:08:43.050 --> 01:08:45.513
I don't think so.
- Got it. Thank you.

01:08:47.163 --> 01:08:48.750
- [Female Participant 4] My
question is really related,

01:08:48.750 --> 01:08:50.958
it's kind of, I guess similar to what

01:08:50.958 --> 01:08:54.720
that the Lutheran organization asked you.

01:08:54.720 --> 01:08:58.740
I'm wondering, I know
there's a lot of people

01:08:58.740 --> 01:09:02.751
I think listening to this
that are involved in some way,

01:09:02.751 --> 01:09:06.960
shape or form refugee
resettlement or integration,

01:09:06.960 --> 01:09:08.670
which is a phrase I don't
like 'cause it means

01:09:08.670 --> 01:09:11.730
integration into American
poverty, but in this room,

01:09:11.730 --> 01:09:13.140
both in person and online.

01:09:13.140 --> 01:09:15.720
So, I'm wondering what,

01:09:15.720 --> 01:09:18.172
I'm sure this would be
an elongated response,

01:09:18.172 --> 01:09:20.174
but if there's anything you could share

01:09:20.174 --> 01:09:22.950
that you shared with the organization

01:09:22.950 --> 01:09:24.420
that reached out to you and kind of what

01:09:24.420 --> 01:09:29.420
would be the role of
those NGOs to advocate

01:09:29.940 --> 01:09:32.130
for if the violence is with the state

01:09:32.130 --> 01:09:34.920
to be part of, you know, upending that.

01:09:34.920 --> 01:09:36.480
- Yeah, so there's, you know, so the,

01:09:36.480 --> 01:09:39.744
what my response to them
was that I would be really

01:09:39.744 --> 01:09:42.539
uncomfortable to come up
with those answers on my own.

01:09:42.539 --> 01:09:44.520
So, what I suggested was a series where

01:09:44.520 --> 01:09:46.650
we actually discussed
with key policy holders,

01:09:46.650 --> 01:09:48.900
people who are interested
from across the country

01:09:48.900 --> 01:09:51.240
to come in sort of communion
and have a conversation.

01:09:51.240 --> 01:09:53.250
But the way I also approach it was to say

01:09:53.250 --> 01:09:54.870
there's like three levels of this,

01:09:54.870 --> 01:09:55.950
three levels that you can think

01:09:55.950 --> 01:09:58.230
about in terms of how we can reform.

01:09:58.230 --> 01:10:00.060
One is like the things that people can do

01:10:00.060 --> 01:10:02.520
with the resources that
they have at the moment.

01:10:02.520 --> 01:10:05.370
So, that's like using
volunteers for instance,

01:10:05.370 --> 01:10:09.673
for things like tutoring,
for things like, you know,

01:10:09.673 --> 01:10:11.460
you know, online language,

01:10:11.460 --> 01:10:13.740
things that you don't need
sustainability for, for instance.

01:10:13.740 --> 01:10:14.970
And not using them for things

01:10:14.970 --> 01:10:17.045
that people crucially need, for instance.

01:10:17.045 --> 01:10:19.530
Thinking about the rhetoric
that we use, right?

01:10:19.530 --> 01:10:22.110
Is self sufficiency, when we
talk about self sufficiency,

01:10:22.110 --> 01:10:23.850
do we talk about it as like the goal?

01:10:23.850 --> 01:10:24.986
Do we say things like refugees need

01:10:24.986 --> 01:10:26.880
to learn to be independent?

01:10:26.880 --> 01:10:29.610
Or do we say, you know, this
is a really problematic policy,

01:10:29.610 --> 01:10:31.470
we are gonna help you to
the extent that we are able

01:10:31.470 --> 01:10:34.110
within the framework of
what we can do, right?

01:10:34.110 --> 01:10:36.900
Which is a very different
approach to the same question.

01:10:36.900 --> 01:10:39.390
Do we actually recognize,
I mean, you know,

01:10:39.390 --> 01:10:43.080
unfortunately even of a
well-meaning organization has people

01:10:43.080 --> 01:10:47.220
within their midst that are
just really problematic, right?

01:10:47.220 --> 01:10:49.560
And so do we think about,
do we train our staff?

01:10:49.560 --> 01:10:51.420
Do we have conversations
about what it means

01:10:51.420 --> 01:10:54.180
to be, you know, respectful?

01:10:54.180 --> 01:10:55.920
What it means to treat
people with dignity.

01:10:55.920 --> 01:10:58.740
How you receive people, how
you speak to people, right?

01:10:58.740 --> 01:10:59.967
Is that a part of how we sort

01:10:59.967 --> 01:11:02.437
of identify and understand ourselves?

01:11:02.437 --> 01:11:05.670
Do we talk about, do we
essentialize countries?

01:11:05.670 --> 01:11:07.080
Do we say Iraqi are like this

01:11:07.080 --> 01:11:08.520
and Palestinians are like that?

01:11:08.520 --> 01:11:09.600
Just something that I heard said,

01:11:09.600 --> 01:11:11.250
and Syrians are like this, right?

01:11:11.250 --> 01:11:13.050
Do we use those kinds of language?

01:11:13.050 --> 01:11:15.990
Is that allowed and accepted
within our organization?

01:11:15.990 --> 01:11:17.850
Do we have opportunities for people

01:11:17.850 --> 01:11:19.080
to learn English in the evenings?

01:11:19.080 --> 01:11:22.110
Do we have opportunities for
people to learn English online?

01:11:22.110 --> 01:11:23.280
Do we give rides to people?

01:11:23.280 --> 01:11:25.380
I mean, there's just like a
million different examples,

01:11:25.380 --> 01:11:27.090
both from an instrumental perspective,

01:11:27.090 --> 01:11:29.820
but also from a rhetoric
and a language perspective.

01:11:29.820 --> 01:11:32.610
The second, the meta level
of this is to think about,

01:11:32.610 --> 01:11:35.640
okay, what do we wanna fundraise for?

01:11:35.640 --> 01:11:38.190
What are we not doing now that we can,

01:11:38.190 --> 01:11:41.880
that we want resources
to be able to do, right?

01:11:41.880 --> 01:11:44.040
And the third level is how do we advocate?

01:11:44.040 --> 01:11:45.030
What are our policy points

01:11:45.030 --> 01:11:46.350
when we're advocating with the state?

01:11:46.350 --> 01:11:48.960
What do we tell our lobbyists to say?

01:11:48.960 --> 01:11:51.450
And so my goal is to work
with this organization

01:11:51.450 --> 01:11:53.730
to be like, okay, if
you're actually interested,

01:11:53.730 --> 01:11:57.450
let's work together and find
what these three things are,

01:11:57.450 --> 01:11:59.160
what these three buckets of things are,

01:11:59.160 --> 01:12:01.886
sort of dreams of changing the system,

01:12:01.886 --> 01:12:05.070
dreams that are fundable and dreams

01:12:05.070 --> 01:12:07.570
that don't need funding
and can be done right now.

01:12:08.624 --> 01:12:11.804
- [Leslie] So that, so unfortunately,

01:12:11.804 --> 01:12:15.261
that is all the time
that we have right now.

01:12:15.261 --> 01:12:17.717
I invite those of you who are in the room

01:12:17.717 --> 01:12:20.550
to stay after and just
have informal conversation

01:12:20.550 --> 01:12:22.500
with Heba for a few minutes.

01:12:22.500 --> 01:12:24.921
But on behalf of the
Program for Refugees Study

01:12:24.921 --> 01:12:28.770
or for Refugees Versus
Displacement Humanitarian Response,

01:12:28.770 --> 01:12:30.110
and the Center for Race,

01:12:30.110 --> 01:12:32.970
Indigeneity and Transnational Migration,

01:12:32.970 --> 01:12:34.620
I just wanna say thank you to all of you

01:12:34.620 --> 01:12:36.672
and especially thank you to Heba for this.

01:12:36.672 --> 01:12:39.672
(crowd applauding)

